WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.700
Qwen.

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.348
Alibaba's open family, and the most fine-tuned lineage after Llama.

3
00:00:06.348 --> 00:00:08.100
Here is what sets it apart.

4
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.200
Three things distinguish Qwen.

5
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.345
First, multilingual strength.

6
00:00:13.345 --> 00:00:18.500
It was trained heavily beyond English, especially Indic and East Asian languages.

7
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.700
Second, it is consistently strong at coding and math.

8
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.467
Third, most sizes are Apache licensed, which matters when your fine-tune ships in a product.

9
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:31.800
The second advantage is a complete ladder of sizes.

10
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:37.800
Half a billion to three billion parameters runs on edge devices, even a Raspberry Pi.

11
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.600
Seven to fourteen billion are the fine-tuning workhorses, on one GPU.

12
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.000
Thirty-two billion and up are near-frontier flagships.

13
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:50.300
And variants for code, vision, and embeddings fill every other slot.

14
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.333
Let's test the multilingual claim.

15
00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:56.900
We load a small one and a half billion parameter Qwen.

16
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.833
Then ask a question in Hindi, about the difference between a satellite and a meteor.

17
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:05.300
Run the same prompt through Llama, and the gap is the point.

18
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.262
Here is a real scenario.

19
00:01:08.262 --> 00:01:13.767
A telemetry operation ingests reports in Hindi and Tamil alongside English.

20
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:17.400
The Llama-based triage model mangles the Indic scripts.

21
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:23.300
The same LoRA recipe on Qwen handles them natively, with no translation layer.

22
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.288
No extra latency either.

23
00:01:25.288 --> 00:01:30.633
One afternoon of side-by-side prompting saved a whole preprocessing service.

24
00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:33.733
The practical takeaway is simple.

25
00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:42.800
If your task involves non-English text, or needs one family across many sizes, Qwen is usually the first checkpoint to benchmark.

26
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:44.819
Let's recap.

27
00:01:44.819 --> 00:01:48.367
Qwen is strong at multilingual work, code, and math.

28
00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:50.133
It has a full size ladder.

29
00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:52.433
Most sizes are Apache licensed.

30
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:55.400
Benchmark it first for non-English work.

31
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.400
Next up: Gemma.
